<<
/Title ("Arduino" encompasses both the hardware boards and the software development environment, with the Arduino IDE serving as a user-friendly interface for coding and uploading programs to the board. At the heart of the Arduino Uno lies the ATmega328P microcontroller, which features 16 MHz processing power, 32 KB of flash memory for code storage, 2 KB of SRAM for data storage, and 1 KB of EEPROM for non- volatile storage. This microcontroller forms the foundation of the Arduino Uno's extensive capabilities. The board includes 14 digital I/O pins, 6 of which support PWM functionality, and 6 analog input pins, facilitating interaction with external sensors, actuators, and displays. Additionally, the Arduino Uno features a USB interface for seamless connection to computers, enabling effortless programming and communication, as well as a reset button for quick troubleshooting and built-in LEDs for visual feedback.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1839 0 R
/Prev 1847 0 R
/Next 1848 0 R
>>
endobj
1854 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Arduino Uno's versatility finds expression in various domains, from prototyping electronic projects to home automation, education, art, design, IoT applications, and robotics. In educational settings, it serves as a practical tool for teaching electronics and programming concepts. Its straightforward design allows students to quickly grasp the basics and start building their projects. For hobbyists, the Arduino Uno is a cornerstone for DIY projects, ranging from home automation systems to interactive art installations. Its compatibility with numerous shields add-on boards that extend its functionality further enhances its capabilities. Shields can provide additional features such as motor control, internet connectivity, and GPS tracking, making the Arduino Uno a robust foundation for complex projects.)
