/Title (There are two main types of buzzers used in electronics: active buzzers and passive buzzers. An active buzzer is a self-contained unit that includes a built-in oscillator. This means that when we apply a steady DC voltage to it, it will immediately produce a fixed-frequency sound. It's very easy to use because we simply turn it on or off, similar to an LED. A passive buzzer, on the other hand, does not have its own internal oscillator. To produce a sound, we must provide it with a square-wave signal of a specific frequency. This gives we much more control over the sound. We can change the frequency of the square wave to produce different tones, allowing for a variety of sounds like musical notes or more complex alert patterns. For most simple projects that just need a basic beep, an active buzzer is the easiest choice. For projects that require different tones or melodies, a passive buzzer is a better option.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1914 0 R
/Prev 1916 0 R
/Next 1926 0 R
>>
endobj
1921 0 obj
<<
/Title (The basic working principle of a buzzer is quite straightforward. Most buzzers use the piezoelectric effect . A piezoelectric buzzer contains a small disc made of a special ceramic material. When an electrical voltage is applied to this disc, it vibrates rapidly. This vibration creates pressure waves in the air, which our ears perceive as sound. The frequency of the sound \(how high or low the pitch is\) depends on how quickly the voltage changes, and the loudness depends on the voltage's strength. This simple mechanism makes buzzers reliable, compact, and energy-efficient.)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1914 0 R
/Prev 1926 0 R
/Next 1917 0 R
>>
endobj
